			"Message": "fix(Wind): Align FileSystemProviderError names with VS Code's token format\n\nWind's FileSystemErrorCode uses key-like values ('FileNotFound', 'FileExists'), but VS Code's toFileSystemProviderErrorCode extracts the token from error.name and switches on 'EntryNotFound', 'EntryExists', etc. Add a mapping function VsCodeErrorCodeToken to translate between the two formats. Also set this.name to the '<code> (FileSystemError)' pattern that VS Code's markAsFileSystemProviderError() expects. Remove individual name assignments from subclasses since the base class now handles naming.\n\nWithout this fix, mkdirp and validateWriteFile treat missing-file/dir errors as unknown and rethrow, surfacing as unhandled rejections like 'Unable to write file .../tasks.log (Error: Failed to stat file ...)'.\n\nThis is a coordinated update with Cocoon (submodule sync).",
			"SHA": "1a7667bfc7b1461b709cc31a642cd76a71431b93"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T00:28:58Z",
			"File": [
				"⡟⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				4
			],
			"Message": "fix(Project): Coordinate extension compatibility and IPC channel fixes\n\nFour related fixes improve extension host integration and IPC routing:\n\n- **Cocoon:** Add missing TextEditorCursorStyle (.Line, .Block, etc.) and UIKind (.Desktop, .Web) enums that extensions like vscodevim and gitlens depend on at activation. Also add stub terminal shell-integration event handlers to prevent crashes.\n\n- **Mountain:** Return correct Cocoon PID via GetCocoonPid() instead of Mountain's own PID. The renderer needs this to correlate extension-host debug adapters with the actual Node.js process. Also register walkthrough commands as no-ops for claude-code extension.\n\n- **Output:** Fix CatchOutputFolderRejection regex to match both `() => undefined` and minified `() => void 0`. Correct userDataSync channel names: `userDataSyncService` → `userDataSync`, `userDataSync` → `userDataSyncAccount` per VS Code source.\n\n- **Wind:** Sync userDataSync IPC channel names to match Output and VS Code registration.\n\nImpact: Extensions with cursor styling and UI detection now activate without crashes, debug adapters attach to the correct process, and user data sync IPC routes correctly.",
			"SHA": "93496cae35dfe1651b356c15770bbe7ae25f0dd5"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-23T00:02:12Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Output): Fix binary file corruption in FileProtocolShim\n\nThe file:read Tauri command was incorrectly invoked with named-object args ({ path, encoding: \"utf8\" }) and the response was assumed to be a string. Mountain actually returns raw bytes as { buffer: number[] } and ignores the encoding param entirely. When VS Code loaded binary files (WASM, fonts, images), the response body became \"[object Object]\" which corrupted the data. This broke TextMate syntax highlighting with: \"WebAssembly.Module doesn't parse at byte 0: module doesn't start with '\\0asm'\".\n\nFix by: 1) Invoking file:read with positional array args [path] as expected by MountainIPCInvoke 2) Adding UnwrapReadResult() to unwrap { buffer: number[] } into Uint8Array for binary-safe Response bodies 3) Updating content type to Uint8Array | string | Blob | null. All three protocol handlers (VSCodeFileHandler, VSCodeUserDataHandler, FileHandler) now use the binary-safe pattern.\n\nThis restores TextMate grammar loading and binary asset handling.",
			"SHA": "5864c4e9c32ce3903e9f1ec3efae89b372751099"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-22T23:44:51Z",
			"File": [
				"⠇⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				2
			],
			"Message": "build(Project): Coordinate Element submodule updates across 2 components\n\nSynchronize Mountain and Output submodules with the following changes:\n- **Mountain:** Expand v2.1 changelog with April 17–22 commits (documentation-only update).\n- **Output:** Add CatchOutputFolderRejection transform plugin — Step 15 in the build pipeline catches an unhandled promise rejection from VS Code's outputServices.createFolder(). In Land, the VFS-IPC stat call rejects with FileNotFound before fileService can swallow it, causing the rejection to propagate past the .then(() => undefined) handler and surface as telemetry noise in PostHog. The plugin uses regex match against outputServices.js to append .catch(() => undefined) to the createFolder chain, silencing the harmless rejection since the output folder is created lazily on first channel write.\n\nThis coordinated update delivers documentation refresh and telemetry noise reduction while maintaining identical runtime behavior.",
			"SHA": "0ecb3ed18924a00c4ab770e021efb0da418d50b7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-04-22T23:44:18Z",
			"File": [
				"⡇⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				28,
				3
			],
			"Message": "fix(Project): Distinguish user vs built-in extensions, add IPC stubs\n\nCoordinate fix across Mountain, Output, and Wind submodules:\n\n- **Mountain:** Add `IsUserExtensionScanPath()` helper to detect whether a scan directory represents the user-writable `~/.land/extensions` root versus bundled paths like `Resources/extensions` and VS Code's submodule. Previously every extension was marked `IsBuiltin = true` regardless of source, causing user-installed VSIXes to appear under \"Built-in\" in the Extensions sidebar and leaving `@installed` (which filters for User-scope) empty.\n\n- **Output/Wind:** Add missing stub handlers in TauriMainProcessService to prevent renderer crashes when VS Code code calls unimplemented services: extension tips (empty arrays), MCP management (empty arrays), user data sync (status tuple), and language detection (empty extensions). These stubs return safe empty values matching the absence of corresponding backends in Land.\n\nImpact: User VSIX extensions now appear correctly under \"Installed\" (not \"Built-in\") and VS Code workbench code gracefully handles the lack of sync/tips/MCP/language detection backends without crashing.",
			"SHA": "2af35a6c2d381bb04045e0d3344f14f1fe92a056"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1779985059204
}